    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm having an issue with some walnut I'm working with for a cabinet project. I'm cutting dovetails by hand, but I keep getting these small splinters and tear-outs right at the tips of the pins. It's ruining the fit. Any idea what I might be doing wrong with my chisel or my grain direction?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Dovetailing can be a bit of a challenge, especially when those pesky splinters and tear-outs start to appear. It's not uncommon for the tips of the pins to be particularly prone to these issues, especially if you're working with hardwoods like walnut. You might be experiencing a bit of a \"gobbling\" problem, where the chisel is eating away at the wood too aggressively, leaving behind those pesky splinters. \n\nTo address this, try adjusting your chisel angle and pressure. A more gentle, controlled motion can help prevent the chisel from getting too deep and causing those unwanted tear-outs. Also, pay attention to the grain direction – if the wood is growing in a direction that's perpendicular to the cut, it can be more prone to splitting and tear-outs. \n\nOne technique that might help is to use a \"goblin's grip\" on your chisel, where you hold it at a slightly more acute angle than usual, with the bevel facing the direction of the cut. This can help you maintain more control over the chisel and prevent those unwanted splinters.",
      "source": "target",
